Let's not overrule; it's pre-existing and perhaps Angular's isolate scope directive only creates isolate scope for its own template, while other directives on same element... Actually I recall exactly this: "If a directive creates an isolate scope, it is only available to the directive's template, not to other directives on the same element" is **not** true. Angular docs: "An isolate scope does not prototypically inherit from the parent scope... The isolate scope is available only to the directive that requested it and its template." Hmm, perhaps other directives on same element get the isolate scope too, but directives like ng-click are not in the template. Let me recall common issue: `<input ng-model="x" my-directive>` where my-directive has isolate scope; ngModel receives isolate scope? Yes, all directives on element share. So likely pre-existing `acordes.html` works because maybe `ng-click` is on the parent? It isn't.

